### 5.1 Log contract

> A baseline requirement for any long-running service, on the same level as "handle errors explicitly" and "secrets come only from environment variables". Write to this from day one; do not wait until monitoring is needed and then go back to rework it. Section 4 of `CLAUDE.md` is the condensed version; this is the full version.

**Shape**: one JSON object per line, written to stdout (containerized services are collected and rotated by the runtime; do not write log files); every line has `time` (RFC3339, milliseconds), `level` (`debug` / `info` / `warn` / `error`, lowercase), and `msg` (a stable short phrase with no variables embedded; variables always go in separate fields). `msg` is the interface: monitoring and troubleshooting recognize lines by it, so changing it changes the interface. One line is at most 4 KB; put stack traces in a `stack` field.

**Required event lines** (`msg` is fixed; the English phrases below are not translated):

| When | Level | `msg` | Fields |
|---|---|---|---|
| Before the process starts listening | info | `service start` | `service`, `version` (short commit hash injected at build time), `addr` |
| Process exit (signal, listen error, startup failure) | info / error | `service stop` | `reason`; on failure also `err` |
| Each HTTP request ends | info (error for 5xx) | `http request` | `method`, `route` (the route template, not the actual path), `path`, `status`, `duration_ms`, `request_id`; add `user_id` when logged in; health-check paths are not logged |
| A panic / unhandled exception is caught | error | `panic` | `err`, `stack`, `request_id`; if the response was already written, only log the line and abort the connection |
| A scheduled / background job with an interval > 1 minute starts | info | `job start` | `job`, `run_id`, `trigger` (timer / manual / retry / startup) |
| A job ends | info (error on failure) | `job end` | `job`, `run_id`, `ok`, `duration_ms`, per-kind counts, `err` on failure; a partial failure is also `ok=false` |
| A tick of high-frequency polling (interval <= 1 minute; once a minute counts) | — | log `job end` only when something was done or an error occurred; one `job heartbeat` line every 5 minutes | `job`, `ticks`, counts since the last heartbeat, `skipped`, `errors` |
| A job retry | warn (error on giving up) | `job retry` | `job`, `attempt`, `max_attempts`, `next_retry_in_s`; on giving up `attempts`, `giving_up=true` |
| A call to an external service (LLM, third-party API, database migration) | info (warn / error on failure) | `<target> call` (e.g. `llm call`, `payment call`) | `target`, `ok`, `status` (HTTP status code; 0 or null if unavailable), `duration_ms`, `attempt` or `attempts`; never log the prompt, request body, response body, or tokens |

**Dedup**: when the same cause keeps failing, log the first error with a given error text only; afterwards drop to debug or carry a `repeat` count; reset once it recovers. Never repeat the identical error every N minutes. Dedup applies only to repeated error-description lines for the same cause; `http request` and `<target> call` lines are logged every time, at their normal level.

**Health checks**: `GET /health` has liveness semantics, always HTTP 200, with body `{ok, version, db, uptime_s}` and `ok=false` when a dependency is broken; readiness is a separate `GET /health/ready` that returns 503 when a dependency is unavailable, and the container health check probes it. Neither path writes an access line.

**Never log**: secrets, tokens, cookies, Authorization headers, passwords, raw emails and phone numbers (mask them if you must, `t***@domain`), user-authored content, LLM prompts and response bodies. Error text counts too: `fmt.Errorf("lookup user (%s)", email)`, which splices an email into the error, violates this just the same.

**Version injection**: Dockerfile `ARG APP_VERSION=unknown` + `ENV APP_VERSION=$APP_VERSION`, and CI builds pass `--build-arg APP_VERSION=<short commit hash>`; at runtime the version is taken from `APP_VERSION`, then the vcs revision in the build info, then `unknown`.

**Acceptance checklist** (run through it once before a new project's first release):

1. After startup, everything on stdout is JSON lines.
2. Request any endpoint: one extra `http request` line appears and `route` is a template.
3. `service start` carries the commit.
4. Each job, after one run, shows a `job end` (high-frequency ones show a `job heartbeat`).
5. Make an external call fail: you see a `<target> call` line with `err` and no secrets in it.
6. The `/health` body has all four fields, and `/health/ready` returns 503 when the dependency is cut off.
7. Grepping the logs finds no emails, tokens, or passwords.

## 6. Directory Layout

Follows Go community conventions (`cmd/` entry point + `internal/` private packages):

```
{{PROJECT_NAME}}/
├── Dockerfile                      # Multi-stage: Go compile + frontend build (see Section 7)
├── backend/
│   ├── go.mod  go.sum
│   ├── cmd/
│   │   └── server/
│   │       └── main.go             # Entry point: wires up routing, pgx connection pool, static frontend, /health
│   ├── internal/
│   │   ├── config/                 # Config (reads env vars)
│   │   ├── db/                     # pgxpool connection pool
│   │   ├── repo/                   # Hand-written pgx repositories (one file per entity, typed queries)
│   │   ├── handlers/               # REST handlers (corresponding to Section 5), one file per resource
│   │   ├── service/                # Business logic
│   │   ├── model/                  # Domain structs
│   │   └── middleware/             # Optimistic-lock conflict -> 409, JSON errors, request logging
│   ├── migrations/                 # golang-migrate plain SQL
│   └── scripts/                    # Test scripts etc.
└── frontend/                       # If needed: React + TS + Vite
    ├── src/
    │   ├── pages/
    │   ├── components/
    │   ├── api/                    # REST client
    │   └── App.tsx
    ├── package.json
    └── vite.config.ts
```

## 7. Deployment

- Shape: single container (port 8080, `/health` for liveness, `/health/ready` for readiness; body and semantics in 5.1 Log contract); frontend build output is served statically by the Go process (`embed.FS` or a static directory)
- Build: Docker multi-stage — `node:20-alpine` builds the frontend → `golang:1.25-alpine` compiles (`CGO_ENABLED=0`, embeds the frontend dist) → `alpine:3.20` minimal runtime image
- Horizontal scaling: Go instances are stateless and can run multiple replicas sharing the same database

